## Musculoskeletal Treatment in Mariánské Lázně

Painful joints, a stiff back and rheumatic inflammation are the most common reasons people come to Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad). Treatment of the musculoskeletal system has a two-hundred-year tradition here and rests on three local natural resources: peat from the Slavkov Forest, mineral springs with natural carbon dioxide and the climate at 630 metres above sea level. Added to these are physiotherapy, pool exercise and medical supervision throughout the stay.

This article explains which diagnoses are treated here, how the individual treatments are carried out and what controlled studies say about their effect. The aim of spa treatment is not to restore cartilage, but to ease pain and stiffness for months and improve mobility; that is exactly what the research documents for a number of diagnoses. Inclusion in the treatment plan is decided by the spa physician after the initial examination.

## Which Diagnoses Are Treated Here

The Czech indication list (Decree No. 2/2015 Coll.) places diseases of the musculoskeletal system in group VII. Among others, it includes:

- **VII/1–3** — rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis (Bekhterev's disease) and other inflammatory rheumatic diseases systematically treated as an outpatient
- **VII/5** — osteoporosis with complications
- **VII/6** — painful syndromes of tendons, tendon insertions and muscles, including occupational overuse
- **VII/7–8** — osteoarthritis of the hips and knees under the care of an orthopaedist and rehabilitation physician; osteoarthritis in other locations
- **VII/9** — chronic vertebrogenic pain syndrome (back pain) of functional origin
- **VII/10–11** — conditions after orthopaedic surgery with joint replacement, after injuries and after spinal surgery

For each item the list specifies the type of care and the length of stay. For example, osteoarthritis of the knee or hip (VII/7) has a basic stay of 21 days in both comprehensive and contributory care, rheumatoid arthritis (VII/1) a basic stay of 28 days in comprehensive care, and conditions after joint replacement (VII/10) 28 days. The full overview including lengths of stay can be found on the page [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications).

Inflammatory rheumatic diseases are treated during quiet periods of the disease, not during a flare. The timing is determined by the rheumatologist.

## What the Research Says

Spa treatment of the musculoskeletal system is one of the best-studied areas of balneology. The evidence, however, is not equally strong for every diagnosis.

### Osteoarthritis of the Knee and Hip

A systematic review by Forestier et al. concluded that spa therapy (mineral baths, peloids, showers, exercise) relieves pain and improves function in knee osteoarthritis, and that the effect is clinically meaningful for **3 to 6 months, sometimes up to 9 months** ([Forestier et al., 2016](https://consensus.app/papers/details/163b339826a2536eb7f05ce9b9111754/), *Ann Phys Rehabil Med*). The ANGELLO randomised trial with 145 patients aged 50–80 confirmed that three weeks of spa treatment improved function, pain and quality of life, and that the difference from usual care persisted at 6 months ([Forestier et al., 2025](https://consensus.app/papers/details/ec2bd40091555fb8918ed883777a3427/), *Int J Biometeorol*). A meta-analysis of 17 studies found better long-term quality of life compared with standard treatment and less pain compared with placebo ([Antonelli et al., 2018](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00296-018-4081-6), *Rheumatol Int*).

Whether the mineral water itself acts, or only warmth and rest, was addressed by a double-blind Hungarian study: underwater massage in mineral water for 3 weeks improved range of motion, function and quality of life more than the same treatment in plain water ([Hanzel et al., 2018](https://consensus.app/papers/details/28592fd629bd5a1daed14667a4e3715c/), *Int J Biometeorol*).

### Chronic Back Pain

Two French randomised trials from the 1990s followed three-week spa treatment for chronic low back pain. After the cure, spinal mobility, pain and medication use improved; pain relief and lower medication use persisted for 6 to 9 months, while the functional score returned to baseline ([Guillemin et al., 1994](https://consensus.app/papers/details/ae6044021e235aad9e5bb9e81e6e18c1/), *Br J Rheumatol*; [Constant et al., 1995](https://consensus.app/papers/details/c40f2f7cb25b57788b900083c0151358/), *J Rheumatol*).

### Ankylosing Spondylitis and Rheumatoid Arthritis

For ankylosing spondylitis the evidence is fairly strong: in a Dutch randomised trial with 120 patients, a three-week spa programme with exercise improved function and quality of life over the full 40 weeks of follow-up and was cost-effective ([van Tubergen et al., 2002](https://consensus.app/papers/details/0e1611a811bd5155a8737b08699663d0/), *Arthritis Rheum*). In rheumatoid arthritis the spa stay complements the drug treatment led by the rheumatologist. In a randomised crossover trial with 50 patients on disease-modifying drugs, adding two weeks of spa treatment improved disease activity and the global assessment of both patient and physician, and the difference lasted up to 6 months ([Karagülle et al., 2018](https://consensus.app/papers/details/3a546f1db0215be397a42844e0a18000/), *Int J Biometeorol*).

### Fibromyalgia

A meta-analysis of 16 randomised trials showed that balneotherapy reduces pain and disability in fibromyalgia and that the effect lasts up to 6 months ([García‑López et al., 2024](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00484-024-02732-3), *Int J Biometeorol*).

## Peat Wraps

The peat is extracted in the Slavkov Forest near the town. It contains humic acids, fulvic acids and mineral salts and has low thermal conductivity, so it releases heat slowly and evenly.

### How a Wrap Is Applied

Peat heated to 42–46 °C is applied in a layer 3–5 cm thick to the affected area, most often the back, knees, hips or shoulders. The patient is wrapped in a sheet and covered. The wrap lasts 20 minutes, followed by 20–30 minutes of rest. During a three-week stay, 8–12 wraps are usually applied on alternate days.

### What Happens during It

Warming the tissues dilates blood vessels, eases muscle tension and reduces the sensitivity of painful nerve endings. In a randomised trial with 52 patients with knee osteoarthritis, nine wraps over 21 days lowered the level of the pro-inflammatory interleukin 1β and raised the anti-inflammatory interleukin 10, while the common inflammatory marker CRP did not change ([Klemm et al., 2020](https://consensus.app/papers/details/c2e6dca1afb85a9384046b83cae52900/), *Phys Med Rehab Kuror*). Another randomised trial compared peloid in direct contact with the skin and peloid applied through foil: both groups improved, but skin contact gave better pain and stiffness at 6 months, suggesting that the chemical composition contributes alongside the warmth ([Adıgüzel et al., 2022](https://consensus.app/papers/details/fc7d520b0a8b5dd6ab38038a56e91c44/), *Int J Biometeorol*). In an Italian randomised trial with 80 patients, a two-week cycle of peloids and baths reduced pain, improved function and reduced medication use for a period of 9 months ([Fioravanti et al., 2010](https://consensus.app/papers/details/117ceeafb8b45fcb8ed8113fbdbc701c/), *Am J Phys Med Rehabil*).

Peat wraps are part of the treatment programmes at Ensana Nové Lázně and Ensana Centrální Lázně.

## Mineral Baths

The [mineral springs](/en/mineral-springs) of Mariánské Lázně have a high content of dissolved carbon dioxide. A carbonic bath lasts 20 minutes in water at 34–36 °C. CO₂ penetrating the skin dilates the skin's blood vessels, the warmth reduces muscle tension and the hydrostatic pressure of the water supports venous return. Baths are combined with underwater massage and with group exercise in the pool, where the water takes the load off the joints and allows movements that would hurt on dry land.

## CO₂ Gas Injections

[Gas injections](/en/magazine/co2-gas-injections) are subcutaneous applications of natural carbon dioxide from the Maria Spring, given with a fine needle around a painful joint or section of the spine. It is a traditional Mariánské Lázně treatment that the spa physician includes as a complement to peat wraps, baths and physiotherapy; a standard course comprises 10–12 applications on alternate days. The treatment takes a few minutes, and after application the injection site may be briefly tender or tight.

## Physiotherapy and Movement Rehabilitation

The treatments are always complemented by individual and group physiotherapy according to a plan drawn up by the [spa physician](/en/magazine/spa-treatment-guide):

- **Individual exercise** with a physiotherapist focused on the muscular corset and joint stability
- **Group exercise in the pool** in water at 30–32 °C
- **Electrotherapy** — interferential currents, TENS, magnetotherapy
- **Soft-tissue techniques and mobilisation** — manual release of the fasciae and restoration of joint play

According to most reviews, exercise is the component that carries a large part of the long-term effect. The physiotherapy team communicates in Czech, German and English.

## Post-Operative Rehabilitation

After total hip or knee replacement, after arthroscopy and after spinal surgery, patients usually arrive six to eight weeks after the procedure. The programme combines pool exercise, individual physiotherapy and gentle warm treatments; what matters most is early and regular rehabilitation, which the spa makes possible under daily supervision.

## How Long the Stay Lasts

The length of a covered stay is set by the indication list for the specific diagnosis: 21 days for osteoarthritis and back pain, 28 days for inflammatory rheumatic diseases and conditions after joint replacement; a repeat stay in contributory care is 21 or 14 days. Self-paying guests agree the length with the spa physician; the specialist literature regards 2–3 weeks with 10–21 treatments as the usual length of spa treatment. According to the studies, the effect in osteoarthritis and back pain persists for 3 to 6 months, sometimes up to 9.

## When Spa Treatment Is Not Suitable

The stay is postponed during an acute flare of an inflammatory disease, acute infection, uncompensated heart failure, recent deep vein thrombosis and the other general contraindications to spa care. The final word rests with the treating doctor in the referral and the spa physician at the initial examination.

## Insurance and Spa Referral

For guests insured in the Czech Republic, **comprehensive care** covers treatment, standard accommodation and meals, on the condition of a recommendation from a rheumatologist, orthopaedist, neurologist or general practitioner and approval by the insurer's reviewing physician; in **contributory care** the insurer covers the treatment and the patient pays for accommodation and meals. The step-by-step procedure is described in the [spa treatment insurance guide](/en/magazine/spa-treatment-insurance-guide). A self-paid stay is possible at any time without waiting.

## Why Mariánské Lázně

Peat, carbonic springs and the climatic conditions are all in one place here, the Ensana spa hotels are connected by covered corridors, and the physiotherapy team works with patients every day throughout the stay. Since 2023, Mariánské Lázně has held the status of a climatic spa. These are the practical reasons why patients with musculoskeletal complaints come here; the spa complements the orthopaedist and rheumatologist, it does not replace them.

## What to Prepare For

Before you leave, ask for a summary of your medical records and bring your current examination results (X-ray, MRI, blood tests). On the day of arrival you will have an initial examination with the spa physician, who draws up the treatment plan and adjusts it during the stay according to how you respond. Information on travel and practical matters can be found in the [practical information](/en/practical-info) section.
